    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you compare the thermal conductivity and heat dissipation rates of ceramic brake pads versus semi-metallic pads during high-speed deceleration cycles? I'm specifically looking at how heat soak affects the friction coefficient over sustained heavy braking periods.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Semi-metallic brake pads generally exhibit higher thermal conductivity than ceramic pads, allowing them to distribute heat more effectively across the rotor and dissipate it faster during intense decel-eration cycles. However, this advantage diminishes under prolonged heavy braking where stacked ceramic pads may struggle to manage heat soak, leading to a more significant drop in friction coefficient compared to semi-metallic ones. While ceramic pads offer superior heat tolerance and consistent friction at extreme temperatures, semi-metallics often provide better initial cooling but can suffer from increased brake fade due to overheating their conductive metallic fibers. Overall, your choice depends on balancing the need for rapid heat dissipation against the duration of sustained braking events.",
