    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'm particularly concerned about the \"acting up\" part when the pads get too hot—like when the pedal feels spongy or the braking becomes inconsistent. Do you have any data on the specific temperature thresholds where the friction coefficient starts to drop significantly due to that heat soak in ceramic pads?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Research indicates that ceramic pads begin to experience significant friction coefficient drops, typically known as fade, when rotor temperatures exceed 600°F (315°C). While semi-metallic pads often encounter their own fade issues around similar temperatures due to organic backing breakdown, ceramics are generally engineered to maintain stability up to over 900°F (480°C) under single-event braking. However, during sustained heavy braking where heat soak can push temperatures closer to 700°F (370°C), ceramic performance may eventually degrade similarly if the cooling rate becomes too slow. Consequently, the \"spongy pedal\" sensation you describe is less common in ceramics but more likely in semi-metallic systems once critical thermal limits are breached.",
